<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amadou Bamba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ahmadu Bamba, Cheikh Ahmadou Bamba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amadou Bamba was a Senegalese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teacher and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mouride-brotherhood">Mouride brotherhood</a>. Preaching work, prayer and non-violent devotion, he founded the holy city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touba">Touba</a> in 1887 and drew large numbers of followers, which alarmed the French colonial authorities, who exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> from 1895 to 1902 and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. His order became one of the most powerful religious and economic forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amadou-bamba.jpg" alt="Amadou Bamba" width="320" height="507" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AhmaduBamba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amadou Bamba was born around 1853 in the village of Mbacké in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a>, into a family of long Islamic tradition that kept close ties with royal dynasties. His father, Maam Mor Anta Saly Mbacke, was a marabout of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a> and served as qadi under both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maba-diakhou-ba">Maba Diakhou Ba</a> and the Damel Lat Jor; it is said that the young Bamba pressed him to give up the post, which he thought obliged his father to rule as power required rather than as Islamic law did. His mother was Maam Diaara Bousso, and both parents descended from Maam Mahram Mbacke, founder of Mbacké.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The brotherhood took shape after the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> monarchies, in a time of deep crisis. Bamba founded it in 1883 and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touba">Touba</a> in 1887. He taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacifism">pacifism</a>, hard work and good manners as a struggle against one's own lower instincts, the greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> fought through learning and fear of God rather than weapons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mouride-brotherhood">Mourides</a> hold that in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> 1895 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> appeared to him in a dream at Touba and made him the renewer of his age.</p><p>The French feared his growing following. On 5 September 1895 he appeared before the Privy Council at Saint-Louis, where, his disciples recount, he first prayed two rakat in the governor's office; the council deported him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>, where he spent seven years and nine months writing prayers and poems. A further exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> and long house arrest at Diourbel followed, and stories of his survival drew thousands more to him. By 1910 the French judged him cooperative, and in 1918 he refused the French Legion of Honor offered for enlisting followers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1926 he began work on the great mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touba">Touba</a>, and after his death in 1927 he was buried beside its site; his sons succeeded him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> of the brotherhood. His teaching of work underlay the order's role in the economy: young followers settled marginal land in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> as groundnut planters, sending part of the proceeds to Touba and in time owning the plantations. Millions make the annual pilgrimage to Touba, the Magal, and Bamba Day parades are held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, among them the Jawharu-n-nafis and the Masâalik Al Jinân, are kept in the library of the Great Mosque.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amadou Bamba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1887 the Sufi leader <b>Amadou Bamba</b> founded Touba in Baol, and the region became the heartland of the Mouride brotherhood.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-baol">Kingdom of Baol</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mbacké, in what is now <a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a>. Died in Diourbel, in what is now <a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.79,-15.91&amp;year=1901" aria-label="Mbacké on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4115 1613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amadou Bamba"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4565 1883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4565 1883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.79,-15.91&amp;year=1901">What was here in 1901? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
